{"attack":true,"case":"The target overstates that sky view factor is the single best explanation for the cross-city nocturnal UHI gap, despite its own counterconditions and problem context.\nThe problem asks for the single mechanism that best explains why the night-time gap is larger in some cities than in others. The target names building height and street canyon geometry (via sky view factor) as the strongest modulator. However, the target's own counterconditions concede that wind speed differences may instead explain the cross-city variation, and that anthropogenic heat at night may dominate over stored heat release. Empirically, the nocturnal UHI is widely modulated by population/city size and wind speed; reducing the cross-city mechanism to radiative geometry is therefore not the strongest single mechanism.\ncites: dcfda80ad91d9efa0e7c79335613d04b9eb9ef4b02d2282e09c269d75432b217","premise":"The target's mechanism selection is internally contested by its own counterconditions and the problem's demand for the single best cross-city mechanism.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"dcfda80ad91d9efa0e7c79335613d04b9eb9ef4b02d2282e09c269d75432b217","quote":"The cross-city night-time gap is modulated most strongly by building height and street canyon geometry: deeper canyons reduce sky view factor, trapping longwave radiation and slowing nocturnal cooling, so cities with denser, taller built environments exhibit larger nocturnal UHI gaps."},{"block":"dcfda80ad91d9efa0e7c79335613d04b9eb9ef4b02d2282e09c269d75432b217","quote":"If wind speed differences rather than radiative geometry explain the cross-city variation"}]}